{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.KashaevProdEndpoint","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.KashaevProdEndpoint","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RootsOfUnityProduct"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_reflect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_reflect","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevProdEndpoint.lean#L112-L158","doc":"**The exact reflection `s_k + s_{N−1−k} = −log N`**, for every `k < N`.\n\nWritten on `L_k := ∑_{j=1}^{k} log(2 sin(jπ/N)) = −s_k`, so the statement below\nis `L_k + L_{N−1−k} = log N`.  Splitting `Ico 1 N` at `k+1` and reflecting the\nupper block by `j ↦ N − j` turns it into the lower block for `N−1−k`; the\nendpoint value `log N` is `sum_log_two_sin_endpoint`.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE STRUCTURAL HALF OF `qou-mlgp` (A).  Unlike the endpoint, this is\nexact for every `k` as well as every `N`, so it constrains the whole expansion\nrather than one value.  Substituting the conjectured form\n`s_k = (N/π)Λ(θ) − ½log(2N sin θ) + ε_k` with `θ = kπ/N`, `h = π/N`, and using\n`Λ(π − x) = −Λ(x)`, everything cancels and the reflection collapses to\n\n    ε_k + ε_{N−1−k} = ½[f(θ) + f(θ+h)] − (1/h)·∫_θ^{θ+h} f,     f = log(2 sin),\n\nwhich is the TRAPEZOID remainder on a single cell — an identity, not an\nestimate.  Hence the SYMMETRIC half of (A)'s error is a trapezoid remainder,\n`O(h² csc²θ)` in the interior, already sharper than the `O(1/N)` being claimed,\nand the entire difficulty of (A) sits in the ANTISYMMETRIC half.\n\nMeasured (`computations/probes/kashaev_reflection_probe.py`, 40 digits): the\nidentity holds exactly, the symmetric part is `O(1/N²)`, and the antisymmetric\n`1/N` term is the Euler–Maclaurin `B₂` term `−(π/12N)·cot θ` — vanishing\nidentically at `θ = π/2`, as the antisymmetry forces. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sin_pi_div_reflect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sin_pi_div_reflect","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevProdEndpoint.lean#L102-L110","doc":"The single-factor reflection `sin((N−i)π/N) = sin(iπ/N)`, from\n`sin(π − x) = sin x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_endpoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.sum_log_two_sin_endpoint","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevProdEndpoint.lean#L79-L100","doc":"The same as a statement about `s_k`: `s_{N-1} = −log N`, where\n`s_k = −∑_{j=1}^k log(2 sin(jπ/N))`.  This is the value the refined\nEuler–Maclaurin expansion must return at `k = N-1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.kashaevProd_endpoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.kashaevProd_endpoint","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevProdEndpoint.lean#L58-L77","doc":"**`kashaevProd N (N-1) = N²`** — exact, for every `N ≠ 0`.\n\nThe far endpoint of the Kashaev product in closed form, from\n`∏_{j=1}^{N-1} 2 sin(jπ/N) = N`.  Equivalently `s_{N-1} = −log N`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.kashaev_factor_eq_sin_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FigureEightVolume.kashaev_factor_eq_sin_sq","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/KashaevProdEndpoint.lean#L50-L56","doc":"Each Kashaev factor is a squared sine: `2 - 2cos(2πj/N) = (2 sin(πj/N))²`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}